1
00:00:01,540 --> 00:00:03,170
I've been wanting to see you.

2
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:07,340
That's a surprise.

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:10,300
I didn't expect you to still
feel that way, Hibino-san.

4
00:00:13,510 --> 00:00:14,970
What about you?

5
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:16,240
What do you mean?

6
00:00:16,970 --> 00:00:20,300
Like saying you already
have someone special.

7
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,600
I'm getting old.
That actually hurts a little.

8
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:28,960
We never had to break
up in the first place.

9
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:32,750
We were so good together.

10
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:38,010
I regret it.

11
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:41,200
I shouldn't have left Tokyo.

12
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:58,500
"Love is Better the Second Time Around season2"
(Episode 2)

13
00:01:20,910 --> 00:01:24,290
A university in Tokyo is inviting me over.

14
00:01:25,830 --> 00:01:26,830
What do you think?

15
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:29,279
That's not for me to answer.

16
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:30,540
Do you think I shouldn't go?

17
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:32,480
Of course.

18
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:34,440
I think it'd be great if
you accepted the offer.

19
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:39,240
That way, you and I could...

20
00:01:40,990 --> 00:01:41,990
Excuse me.

21
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:52,400
Professor Hibino.

22
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:54,840
Who might you be?

23
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:57,860
Pardon me.

24
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:01,020
I'm Miyata from Tokyo
Commercial Corporation.

25
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:04,890
What's your relationship with Iwanaga?

26
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:06,790
Oh, right.

27
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:08,540
Senpai and I are...

28
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:15,090
Teacher Iwanaga is featured in our
company's magazine, Business Now.

29
00:02:15,130 --> 00:02:16,820
He writes a column for us.

30
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:18,180
I see.

31
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:19,990
I'll have to check it out next time.

32
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:22,680
I'm Hibino Takaaki.

33
00:02:22,850 --> 00:02:24,100
Nice to meet you.

34
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:26,100
Nice to meet you too.

35
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:36,880
Have you known him for a long time?

36
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:40,380
Yes.

37
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:42,880
You look quite young.

38
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:44,840
Not at all.

39
00:02:44,990 --> 00:02:46,580
I'm already 32.

40
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:51,040
Charming people always look
younger than their actual age.

41
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:52,130
You flatter me.

42
00:02:52,330 --> 00:02:54,840
I know this is a bit forward.

43
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:58,339
But I've always hoped for a chance
to work with you, Professor Hibino.

44
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,470
Oh?

45
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:02,640
What exactly interests you about me?

46
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,079
Hibino-san.

47
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:05,080
Ah.

48
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:06,540
Well.

49
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:11,520
You talked about Japan's agricultural
policies on the morning show today.

50
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:13,080
I found it fascinating.

51
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:16,160
Fascinating, huh?

52
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:23,470
You're quite cute.

53
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:26,960
Thank you for the compliment.

54
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:31,530
I'll be in Tokyo this week
for an academic conference.

55
00:03:32,330 --> 00:03:34,380
If it's tomorrow,
I can make some time.

56
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:36,420
I'd really appreciate that.

57
00:03:36,421 --> 00:03:38,880
I'll call your office later then.

58
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:46,560
You had a guest.
I'm sorry for interrupting.

59
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:51,060
See you at the conference.

60
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:54,150
I'll be going now.

61
00:04:05,370 --> 00:04:08,120
He's even more handsome
in person than on TV.

62
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:10,620
Sorry.

63
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:12,350
Even though we already had plans.

64
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:14,460
If anything, I'm lucky.

65
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:16,040
To have met Professor Hibino.

66
00:04:19,710 --> 00:04:20,710
Senpai.

67
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:23,140
I told you I didn't have
any ulterior motives, right?

68
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:29,420
Regarding chapter three, there's
something I want to discuss with you.

69
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:46,070
Miyata-san.

70
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:55,370
What is it?

71
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:59,590
Do you know who
that handsome older guy is?

72
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:04,169
Professor Hibino from Meisei
University, a prestigious school in Osaka?

73
00:05:04,170 --> 00:05:05,280
Aside from that?

74
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:16,310
You're really not very perceptive, are you?

75
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:19,820
Never mind.

76
00:05:20,150 --> 00:05:24,090
You'll find out sooner or later anyway.

77
00:05:40,130 --> 00:05:43,370
Miyata-san,
Professor Hibino Takaaki is here.

78
00:05:44,090 --> 00:05:45,090
Thank you.

79
00:05:47,430 --> 00:05:49,430
Do you know Professor Hibino?

80
00:05:50,260 --> 00:05:51,260
Yeah.

81
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:54,699
Why didn't you tell us sooner?

82
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:56,600
It's no big deal.

83
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:57,680
Hey.

84
00:05:58,060 --> 00:05:59,190
Okay.

85
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:03,730
Good luck, Deputy Editor-in-Chief.

86
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,070
We're counting on you to save Business Now.

87
00:06:06,110 --> 00:06:07,110
I'll do my best.

88
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:08,760
Okay.

89
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:12,830
Miyata. Miyata.

90
00:06:14,870 --> 00:06:16,120
Excuse me.

91
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:22,420
Thank you for taking
the time to see me today.

92
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,479
Let's get straight to the point.

93
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:26,579
Tell me about yourself.

94
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:27,580
Sure.

95
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:32,550
Our company is planning
a major revamp soon.

96
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:35,699
We'd love to invite you, Professor Hibino,
to write a serialized column for us...

97
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:37,270
That's not what I meant.

98
00:06:39,230 --> 00:06:40,230
Iwanaga.

99
00:06:42,860 --> 00:06:43,860
Huh?

100
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:46,880
You call him "Senpai," right?

101
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:51,070
Ah, how embarrassing.

102
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:56,620
Actually, Teacher Iwanaga and I
went to the same high school.

103
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:00,500
Just like my relationship with Iwanaga.

104
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:02,120
Yes.

105
00:07:03,750 --> 00:07:07,060
Let me tell you a story from the past.

106
00:07:07,170 --> 00:07:08,170
I'm all ears.

107
00:07:10,590 --> 00:07:12,890
I met Iwanaga.

108
00:07:13,550 --> 00:07:17,560
When he was in the doctoral
program at To-Oh University.

109
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:26,460
At the time, I was an assistant professor
in another lab at the same university.

110
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:28,490
Iwanaga?

111
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:32,740
I read the draft of his doctoral thesis
and found it very interesting.

112
00:07:32,990 --> 00:07:34,370
Nice to meet you.

113
00:07:34,490 --> 00:07:35,700
I'm Hibino.

114
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,250
Nice to meet you too.

115
00:07:38,620 --> 00:07:40,290
I'm Iwanaga Takashi.

116
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,550
Thank you for making time to see me today.

117
00:07:49,620 --> 00:07:51,590
Not at all. I should be thanking you.

118
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:53,930
I've read your thesis.

119
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:57,900
That sparked things off, and we
started seeing each other frequently.

120
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:05,710
I was thinking about you
while reading this book.

121
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:09,320
Oh?

122
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:11,320
No, I mean.

123
00:08:11,650 --> 00:08:15,130
I just thought this book
might further support your hypothesis.

124
00:08:17,410 --> 00:08:18,490
Ah.

125
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:25,390
Then one day.

126
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:29,020
We realized how we felt about each other.

127
00:08:49,650 --> 00:08:50,780
Please wait a moment.

128
00:08:52,360 --> 00:08:53,360
Is it you?

129
00:08:53,740 --> 00:08:55,570
The special someone Iwanaga mentioned.

130
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:00,720
I don't know what you're talking about.

131
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:05,470
His taste has certainly changed.

132
00:09:06,940 --> 00:09:12,360
Now that the research subject is clear,
it's easier to formulate a strategy.

133
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:19,970
It's in my nature to get to the
bottom of things if I can't accept them.

134
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:25,600
I'll take my leave now.

135
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:45,500
Welcome back.

136
00:09:47,020 --> 00:09:48,060
I'm home.

137
00:09:52,440 --> 00:09:54,010
Did you meet with Hibino-san?

138
00:09:54,970 --> 00:09:55,970
I did.

139
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:58,920
He promised to consider
the serialized column.

140
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:02,820
I see.

141
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:04,800
That's great.

142
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:07,560
Yeah.

143
00:10:20,070 --> 00:10:23,300
I just made a big fuss
over the watch.

144
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:26,010
If I tell him about today too，

145
00:10:27,383 --> 00:10:30,433
he'll definitely think
I'm a super annoying guy.

146
00:10:44,390 --> 00:10:45,520
What's wrong?

147
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:50,810
Senpai.

148
00:10:55,730 --> 00:10:57,860
Please hold me tight.

149
00:10:57,940 --> 00:10:58,940
Hmm?

150
00:10:59,910 --> 00:11:01,700
Tighter.

151
00:11:06,450 --> 00:11:08,810
He told you about us, didn't he?

152
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:13,940
I would've preferred to
hear it from you, Senpai.

153
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:17,520
I didn't want you to worry unnecessarily.

154
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:25,350
Is Professor Hibino
the one who gave you that watch?

155
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:27,520
No, he's not.

156
00:11:28,890 --> 00:11:32,960
Then what kind of person
is he to you?

157
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:37,440
Hmm... Well.

158
00:11:39,860 --> 00:11:44,120
His words were always profound and sharp.

159
00:11:46,120 --> 00:11:49,200
He strongly stimulated
my thirst for knowledge.

160
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:54,640
To me, he was like a guiding light.

161
00:11:57,170 --> 00:11:58,610
Then why did you break up?

162
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:04,010
He got an associate professor
position at Meisei University.

163
00:12:05,010 --> 00:12:09,060
But I wanted to stay in
Tokyo no matter what.

164
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:16,120
How long did you date?

165
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:22,000
About two and a half years.

166
00:12:26,030 --> 00:12:30,540
That's longer than us.

167
00:12:32,500 --> 00:12:36,350
We're going to spend a lot more time
together in the future, aren't we?

168
00:12:38,230 --> 00:12:39,230
So.

169
00:12:42,050 --> 00:12:43,980
I hope you won't be so jealous.

170
00:12:47,990 --> 00:12:49,090
Jealous...?

171
00:12:50,310 --> 00:12:51,310
But.

172
00:12:53,210 --> 00:12:54,910
I'm actually pretty happy about it.

173
00:12:56,790 --> 00:12:59,110
Please don't make fun of me.

174
00:13:13,190 --> 00:13:14,190
Good night.

175
00:13:21,090 --> 00:13:22,340
Jealous...

176
00:13:25,590 --> 00:13:26,720
Jealous...

177
00:13:30,430 --> 00:13:31,430
Jealous...

178
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:38,360
Jealous...

179
00:13:48,390 --> 00:13:51,700
Am I really jealous?

180
00:13:53,790 --> 00:13:54,790
Miyata.

181
00:13:56,580 --> 00:13:57,780
Miyata.

182
00:13:59,190 --> 00:14:01,690
What happened with Professor Hibino
after that?

183
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:06,090
It seems a bit difficult.

184
00:14:06,130 --> 00:14:08,389
You need to be more proactive.

185
00:14:08,390 --> 00:14:09,870
This is no time to be taking it easy.

186
00:14:09,930 --> 00:14:11,310
Oh, right.

187
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:14,020
Isn't there an academic
conference in Kanda coming up?

188
00:14:14,350 --> 00:14:16,770
Professor Hibino is scheduled
to give a speech there.

189
00:14:16,810 --> 00:14:18,550
You should go too, Miyata.

190
00:14:19,560 --> 00:14:20,560
Huh?

191
00:14:20,561 --> 00:14:23,979
Listen, it's also a good chance to
show your face to other professors.

192
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:24,990
Right?

193
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:27,230
Isn't that great? I'm counting on you.

194
00:14:28,890 --> 00:14:29,890
Understood.

195
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:31,870
Good luck.

196
00:14:40,630 --> 00:14:42,816
(32nd Japan Economic Association)
Let's enter the venue separately.

197
00:14:42,840 --> 00:14:43,750
Huh?

198
00:14:43,751 --> 00:14:45,510
I don't want to mix business with pleasure.

199
00:14:47,010 --> 00:14:48,340
You really make me worry.

200
00:14:48,370 --> 00:14:49,840
I'm not a kid anymore.

201
00:14:50,930 --> 00:14:52,030
See you later then.

202
00:14:55,770 --> 00:14:58,679
Studies show that what truly drives change

203
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:00,036
is the mechanism linked
to compensation itself.

204
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:04,480
What matters isn't the degree of effort, but
the skills cultivated through that effort,

205
00:15:04,550 --> 00:15:07,750
and how they translate
into increased productivity.

206
00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:11,490
In the AI era, we need to rethink

207
00:15:11,530 --> 00:15:16,370
the relationship between human
skill development and economic value.

208
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,390
Thank you all for listening.

209
00:15:24,330 --> 00:15:30,010
After a 30-minute break, Professor
Hibino Takaaki will give his lecture.

210
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:37,020
Please make sure to return to
your seats before the lecture begins.

211
00:15:38,810 --> 00:15:41,020
Trying to tear us apart?

212
00:15:41,850 --> 00:15:43,940
Give it your best shot.

213
00:16:11,610 --> 00:16:13,090
Miyata-san.

214
00:16:21,440 --> 00:16:23,850
You just wore your usual suit?

215
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:28,820
Shiraishi-san, you're looking pretty sharp.

216
00:16:29,740 --> 00:16:34,070
After all, this is a great opportunity
to job hunt and man hunt at the same time.

217
00:16:34,910 --> 00:16:37,990
You're certainly full of energy.

218
00:16:39,990 --> 00:16:44,420
Don't lose either, Miyata-san.

219
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:47,880
Huh?

220
00:16:49,590 --> 00:16:51,400
Don't lose to Professor Hibino.

221
00:16:54,790 --> 00:17:00,720
I don't want to see Teacher Iwanaga
get snatched away by a guy like that.

222
00:17:01,770 --> 00:17:06,150
Are you cheering for me?

223
00:17:08,650 --> 00:17:09,990
Think whatever you want.

224
00:17:10,860 --> 00:17:13,920
I'll take that as a yes.

225
00:17:32,050 --> 00:17:34,609
I'm a bit skeptical if that
method is the most appropriate.

226
00:17:34,610 --> 00:17:36,370
Opinions on that will
probably be polarized.

227
00:17:36,640 --> 00:17:38,679
Sorry to interrupt your conversation.

228
00:17:38,680 --> 00:17:41,450
I'm Miyata Akihiro from
Tokyo Commercial Corporation.

229
00:17:41,770 --> 00:17:45,230
I hope to have the chance
to connect with you all today.

230
00:17:45,390 --> 00:17:46,509
You're just in time.

231
00:17:46,510 --> 00:17:48,360
I was just about to call you over.

232
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:49,900
What's going on?

233
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:52,460
Are you studying me to
come up with a strategy?

234
00:17:53,190 --> 00:17:54,190
No, not at all.

235
00:17:54,610 --> 00:17:55,610
It's my honor.

236
00:17:56,030 --> 00:17:58,370
To actually be the subject
of Professor Hibino's research.

237
00:17:58,700 --> 00:18:00,870
Hibino-san, could the three of us maybe...

238
00:18:00,950 --> 00:18:02,636
We were just discussing the paper published
in last month's academic journal

239
00:18:02,660 --> 00:18:07,200
by Professor Marlowe from Fenwick College.
Have you read it yet?

240
00:18:09,453 --> 00:18:11,701
He used mathematical methods
to delve into previous research,

241
00:18:11,725 --> 00:18:13,973
essentially pioneering
a whole new field.

242
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:18,860
What do you think, Iwanaga?

243
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:25,230
The concept is definitely groundbreaking.

244
00:18:26,320 --> 00:18:27,320
However,

245
00:18:27,321 --> 00:18:29,161
the argument doesn't
seem fully substantiated.

246
00:18:29,640 --> 00:18:30,640
You think so?

247
00:18:30,690 --> 00:18:33,210
I think the argument
is more than sufficient.

248
00:18:33,530 --> 00:18:38,819
I believe drawing that final conclusion based
solely on that premise is a bit of a stretch.

249
00:18:38,820 --> 00:18:41,050
Rather than the main
point, it's more like...

250
00:18:55,540 --> 00:18:56,570
Sorry.

251
00:18:57,080 --> 00:18:58,840
I embarrassed him.

252
00:18:59,220 --> 00:19:01,430
If that's what you think,
then please help me find it.

253
00:19:04,460 --> 00:19:06,170
Can I ask a question?

254
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:09,560
Sure.

255
00:19:10,770 --> 00:19:14,570
What do you guys
usually talk about?

256
00:19:17,570 --> 00:19:18,989
What exactly are you trying to say?

257
00:19:18,990 --> 00:19:22,880
Miyata isn't good enough for you.

258
00:19:26,250 --> 00:19:29,540
We don't have to stress
over promotions anymore.

259
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:31,960
We can finally face each other properly.

260
00:19:34,380 --> 00:19:35,690
Let's start over.

261
00:19:41,060 --> 00:19:42,060
Hibino-san.

262
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:47,680
Back when I said I was staying in Tokyo,

263
00:19:49,140 --> 00:19:52,450
you said it was because I was clinging to
the prestige of To-Oh University, right?

264
00:19:53,110 --> 00:19:54,110
Yeah.

265
00:19:54,610 --> 00:19:58,990
You were just starting out back then, so
it's understandable you'd think that way.

266
00:20:02,280 --> 00:20:03,380
That wasn't it.

267
00:20:07,200 --> 00:20:09,550
The reason I wanted to stay in Tokyo

268
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:16,440
was because Miyata was in this city.

269
00:20:19,240 --> 00:20:24,340
You always make
assumptions about how I feel

270
00:20:25,010 --> 00:20:26,720
and force your answers on me.

271
00:20:30,100 --> 00:20:34,770
I can't stand your
overbearing nature anymore.

272
00:20:37,770 --> 00:20:38,900
I just...

273
00:20:39,820 --> 00:20:42,030
wanted to guide you in the right direction.

274
00:20:42,780 --> 00:20:43,990
Professor Hibino.

275
00:20:47,860 --> 00:20:52,210
I admit I can't debate with
Senpai on the same level.

276
00:20:53,250 --> 00:20:57,210
But I will listen earnestly
to what Senpai has to say,

277
00:20:58,120 --> 00:21:01,960
and organize his thoughts into a format that even
those who don't understand economics can grasp.

278
00:21:03,550 --> 00:21:08,350
Rather than having profound debates
with the person you spend every day with,

279
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:14,980
isn't it much more important
to truly listen to what they have to say?

280
00:21:18,770 --> 00:21:21,610
It seems our opinions differ greatly.

281
00:21:22,030 --> 00:21:23,820
Completely different.

282
00:21:28,320 --> 00:21:31,620
Do you also think that's
the best kind of relationship?

283
00:21:34,790 --> 00:21:35,920
Yes.

284
00:21:39,590 --> 00:21:44,480
Professor Hibino Takaaki's
lecture is about to begin.

285
00:21:45,260 --> 00:21:48,930
Please return to your seats.

286
00:21:50,600 --> 00:21:53,370
To think I wasted my
time on something like this.

287
00:21:55,060 --> 00:21:56,730
I think you know very well

288
00:21:57,280 --> 00:22:01,400
that this man isn't someone
you can easily keep by your side.

289
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:08,370
I wish you both happiness. Excuse me.

290
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:15,000
Keep by my side...

291
00:22:20,420 --> 00:22:22,380
Shall we head back for today?

292
00:22:24,550 --> 00:22:25,760
Sure.

293
00:22:34,350 --> 00:22:35,639
By the way,

294
00:22:35,640 --> 00:22:39,230
how did you know I lived in Tokyo?

295
00:22:39,540 --> 00:22:42,650
I probably heard it from someone
when I went back to my hometown.

296
00:22:43,780 --> 00:22:44,780
So that means...

297
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:48,820
The real reason Senpai and
Hibino-san broke up was actually...

298
00:22:54,100 --> 00:22:59,960
How could I possibly leave you all alone
in this big city?

299
00:23:02,460 --> 00:23:05,020
You really do love me, don't you?

300
00:23:40,300 --> 00:23:44,800
(Sugimoto Kyosuke)

301
00:23:45,670 --> 00:23:48,089
(Next Episode) That's exactly
what I love about you, Miyata.

302
00:23:48,090 --> 00:23:49,160
Senpai.

303
00:23:49,320 --> 00:23:52,179
Another new ex-boyfriend?

304
00:23:52,180 --> 00:23:54,549
Just how many boyfriends
have you had, Senpai?

305
00:23:54,550 --> 00:23:55,819
What does it matter?

306
00:23:55,820 --> 00:23:57,399
I'm jealous.

307
00:23:57,400 --> 00:23:59,520
You're really something else.
